  "notes": "Represents the 'IT field' request \u2014 the general 'Information Sciences and Technology, B.S.' major (ISSWC_BS) was tried first but turned out to have no University Park Suggested Academic Plan (effectively World-Campus-only / not currently offered at any campus), so this major was substituted: it has a real, current, University Park-based 4-year plan. 'Application Focus Selection' slots (9 credits across 3 terms) are chosen from a College-approved list not enumerated in the bulletin's course table. 'etm' marks the courses everything else in the sequence assumes are done. UPDATE after the prereq-parser fix: both STAT 200 and its SCM 200 alternate require MATH 21 (which needs MATH 4, which needs MATH 3), none of which were anywhere in this plan -- added the MATH 3 -> MATH 4 -> MATH 21 chain explicitly (front of Semesters 1-3), which was the true root cause of a long downstream cascade (SRA 231, SRA 365, CYBER 342W, SRA 311, IST 432, CYBER 440 all ultimately need STAT 200). Also dropped the generic, unenumerated 3cr 'Elective' slot (Semester 8) to partially offset the 9 new credits. FLAGGED FOR HUMAN REVIEW: even after that offset (net +6cr), this plan cannot be packed into exactly 8 real terms under the simulator's term-by-term greedy scheduler -- every arrangement tried lands exactly one 3cr item into a 9th term (verified: total credits 129 fit well under the 8-term cap of 138, but the specific 3-credit item granularity of this flowchart's remaining slots leaves each of Semesters 5-8 with only ~2.25cr of unusable slack, never quite enough for one more atomic 3cr course). This is a genuine consequence of the newly-correct STAT 200 prereq chain, not a data error -- CYBER's real published flowchart implicitly assumes an incoming student already has algebra proficiency (skips straight to MATH 110/140), so a student who actually needs the MATH 3/4/21 remediation chain would realistically need a 9th term (or a summer term) in real life. TestCyberPlan.test_full_plan_reaches_graduation_in_four_years (tests.py) hardcodes grad_years=4 and asserts exactly 8 terms with zero warnings -- this assertion may need to change (e.g. to grad_years=5, similar to AE/ARCHBARCH's existing overrides) now that this real prereq gap is enforced; not changed here per instructions not to edit tests.py.",
